Embedded React: render Arc and Svg scenes across the render workers.

Modified from Embedded React (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Cory Lamming) at the
revision pinned in dependencies.lock.json, in the patch order listed there.
Changed files: engine/include/native_renderer.h,
engine/core/{er_parallel.c,renderer_internal.h},
engine/rendering/{arc.c,vector.c}, engine/scene/{compositor.c,er_node_internal.h}
and engine/tests/rendering/test_parallel.c. Not proposed upstream: PS5 React
maintains this change here.

The sliced parallel commit refused any scene holding an Arc or a vector node,
because both rasterizers kept their scratch in statics. Icon-heavy screens
therefore always rendered on one core. Arc span caches become per worker.
Vector rasterization (the tape working set and the edge cache) is bracketed by
an optional lock/unlock pair in EmbeddedRenderWorkers, so with a lock a vector
scene forks and only the vector draws themselves serialize; without one it stays
single-core as before.

Two pieces of per-node bookkeeping were written differently by slices that
disagreed, and are now resolved in er_commit's sequential post-pass:
- A pass that walks a transformed node occluded skips its capture and records
  the raw box. That record is kept aside and used only when no pass painted the
  node, so the painted footprint wins regardless of slice or damage-rect order.
- The one-shot vector/arc sub-region damage flag is cleared after the walk, so
  every slice reads the same rect.

test_parallel checks that an Arc and vector scene forks only with the lock,
takes it, and is byte-identical to a single-core render after a full repaint
and after partial damage.

